<mjml lang="en">
  <mj-head>
    <!--
      PROBE — block-wide Text Color / Link Color overrides (2026-08-24).

      ══════════════════ VERDICTS — ALL CLAIMS MEASURED ══════════════════
      Sent 2026-08-24 as EN templateId 634; EoA test uC5wMRro… (17 clients).
      Read from the delivered HTML and pixel-measured renders.

      C2 LINK HOOK — PASS EVERYWHERE. EN inlined the hook colour onto every
          anchor (delivered: color:#362229 inline, no !important); Word
          renders Earth (pixel-measured 329 Earth / 0 blue in the link run),
          Apple Mail and Gmail render Earth, dark clients force #8CC63F over
          it exactly as pre-flagged. THE LINK COLOR SELECT IS IMPLEMENTABLE
          CATALOG-WIDE.
      C3 BLOCK STYLE — FAILS IN WORD, and the mechanism is now measured:
          EN CONSUMED the block-local style tag (0 tags in delivery) and
          inlined its rules onto every matching element KEEPING !important
          (delivered: style="…color:#F7931E !important…"). CSS clients
          honour that — Apple Mail shows the full override, beating authored
          inline white, and in dark mode the inline !important even beats
          the dark forcing. But Word DROPS an inline declaration that
          carries !important: authored colours return, and the links —
          whose only colour was the dropped declaration — crash to Word
          default blue (pixel-measured 531 blue). A colour control that
          reverts silently in Outlook desktop and breaks its links there is
          not shippable. Block-wide Text Color via block-local style: DEAD.
          (FLAGS A and B were already fatal on their own; the two-instance
          manual step is moot and was not run.)
      C4 CASCADE — text inheritance surprisingly WORKS in Word (Sun body
          copy pixel-confirmed), but the anchor's color:inherit fell to Word
          blue (595 blue), and the catalog stamps colour inline anyway.
          Rejected, now with pixels.

      NEW PORTABLE FACT (recorded in guide §2b): EN's inliner keeps
      !important when inlining BODY-carried style rules — unlike the
      CSS-editor head path, which strips it (2026-08-18) — and Word rejects
      any inline declaration carrying !important. Inlined-!important is
      therefore a Word-only outage switch.
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      Not a catalog page. Build, send dist/probe_block-color-overrides_live.html
      through EN to EoA. Reading order: the DELIVERED HTML first (C3's survival
      is decided there), then light/dark pairs, then the one MANUAL builder
      step at the bottom of this comment.

      THE QUESTION. Can every block offer one "Text Color" and one "Link
      Color" Select that recolors EVERY text site in the block? Three
      mechanisms exist; two flags are already settled WITHOUT sending
      anything, and are recorded here so the verdicts land in context:

      FLAG A (structural, no send needed): a single block-wide TEXT COLOR
      field cannot byte-exactly restore a block whose elements author
      DIFFERENT colours — one field has one default value, and delete-restore
      demands the exact original bytes per element. Block-wide Text Color is
      only ever clean on single-colour blocks, or as a FORCED override that
      admits it cannot restore per-element defaults.

      FLAG B (structural, no send needed): a block-local <style> cannot be
      per-instance. EN blocks carry no instance id, so two copies of the same
      block with different colour picks would both define the same class —
      the later definition wins for both. The manual step below demonstrates
      it live.

      VARIANTS. All four carry the same four text sites: Moss kicker, white
      heading on Evergreen, body copy with an inline link, second paragraph
      with a second link.

        C1 CONTROL      today's authoring. Links render the default
                        stylesheet colour (Evergreen) from the body-carried
                        hook sheet below.
        C2 LINK HOOK    every mj-text also carries class link-earth —
                        simulating ONE Link Color Select whose value token is
                        spliced byte-identically into every css-class slot
                        (the data-link-group sharing precedent). VERDICT: both
                        links Earth #362229 in every light client; dark
                        clients FORCE #8CC63F over it (expected — the dark
                        rules outrank hooks by design; a flag, not a defect);
                        Outlook desktop honours it.
        C3 BLOCK STYLE  the block brings its own <style> (combinator-free,
                        !important) forcing text Sun #F7931E and links Sky
                        #5DD8D8. VERDICTS: (a) the style tag present in the
                        DELIVERED head or body at all; (b) all four sites
                        recoloured in renders, including against inline
                        colours; (c) the manual step below.
        C4 CASCADE      colour on the ancestor, descendants author none.
                        Expected NOT viable — mj-attributes stamps colour
                        inline on real catalog text so inheritance never
                        reaches it, and this variant only exists so the flag
                        cites a render instead of an argument. VERDICT: reads
                        Sun in modern clients; whatever Outlook does is the
                        recorded cost.

      MANUAL STEP (C3, FLAG B): in the EN builder, place this probe's C3
      block TWICE and hand-edit the second copy's <style> to a different
      colour. If both instances render the SECOND colour, the collision is
      demonstrated; if each keeps its own, EN scopes block HTML in a way we
      have not measured and FLAG B needs re-examination.

      Colours: brand palette only (CLAUDE.md → Instrument colors). Sun
      #F7931E = forced text, Sky #5DD8D8 = forced links, Earth #362229 =
      the hook pick, Fern/Evergreen/Moss as authored.
    -->
    <mj-breakpoint width="600px" />
    <mj-title>PROBE — block colour overrides</mj-title>
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-text color="#000000" font-size="18px" font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif" line-height="28px" css-class="wysiwyg" />
      <mj-image padding="0" />
    </mj-attributes>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body background-color="#ffffff">

    <mj-section background-color="#362229" padding="12px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#8CC63F" align="center" padding="0">
          <p style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:3px;font-family:Menlo,Consolas,monospace;">C — BLOCK-WIDE COLOUR OVERRIDES</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- The styles-block mimic: default link colour, the three hooks, and the
         dark forcing — same condition strings as the real sheet. -->
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css">
        a { color: #006837; }
        .link-inverse a { color: #ffffff; }
        .link-earth a { color: #362229; }
        .link-accent a { color: #39b54a; }
        @media only screen and (max-width: 9999px) {
          [data-ogsc] a { color: #8CC63F !important; }
        }
        @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
          a { color: #8CC63F !important; }
        }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>

    <!-- ═══ C1 CONTROL ═══ -->
    <mj-section background-color="#F5FAF1" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p><b>C1 CONTROL</b> — links should be Evergreen (the default hook).</p></mj-text></mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-color="#ffffff" padding="16px 64px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#006837" padding="0 0 4px"><p style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;">MOSS KICKER SITE</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg" container-background-color="#006837" padding="8px"><h2 style="color:#ffffff;margin:0;">White heading site</h2></mj-text>
        <mj-text padding="8px 0 0"><p>Body site with <a href="https://tpl.org">an inline link</a> in it.</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text padding="4px 0 0"><p>Second site, <a href="https://tpl.org">second link</a>.</p></mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ═══ C2 LINK HOOK — the Select-token simulation ═══ -->
    <mj-section background-color="#F7931E" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p><b>C2 LINK HOOK</b> — every text element carries link-earth. BOTH links must be Earth #362229 in light clients; dark clients force #8CC63F (expected).</p></mj-text></mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-color="#ffffff" padding="16px 64px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg link-earth" color="#006837" padding="0 0 4px"><p style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;">MOSS KICKER SITE</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg link-earth" container-background-color="#006837" padding="8px"><h2 style="color:#ffffff;margin:0;">White heading site</h2></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg link-earth" padding="8px 0 0"><p>Body site with <a href="https://tpl.org">an inline link</a> in it.</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg link-earth" padding="4px 0 0"><p>Second site, <a href="https://tpl.org">second link</a>.</p></mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ═══ C3 BLOCK-LOCAL STYLE ═══ -->
    <mj-section background-color="#5DD8D8" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p><b>C3 BLOCK STYLE</b> — this block carries its own style tag. ALL text below must be Sun #F7931E and links Sky #5DD8D8, or the tag did not survive. Check the delivered HTML for the tag itself.</p></mj-text></mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css">
        .probe-c3 p, .probe-c3 h2, .probe-c3 span { color: #F7931E !important; }
        .probe-c3 a { color: #5DD8D8 !important; }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>
    <mj-section background-color="#362229" padding="16px 64px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg probe-c3" color="#006837" padding="0 0 4px"><p style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;">MOSS KICKER SITE</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg probe-c3" container-background-color="#006837" padding="8px"><h2 style="color:#ffffff;margin:0;">White heading site</h2></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg probe-c3" color="#ffffff" padding="8px 0 0"><p>Body site with <a href="https://tpl.org">an inline link</a> in it.</p></mj-text>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg probe-c3" color="#ffffff" padding="4px 0 0"><p>Second site, <a href="https://tpl.org">second link</a>.</p></mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ═══ C4 CASCADE ═══ -->
    <mj-section background-color="#F5FAF1" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p><b>C4 CASCADE</b> — colour on the wrapper only; the copy authors NONE. Sun here proves inheritance reaches it in this client; the catalog cannot use this (mj-attributes stamps colour inline), so this variant exists to make the flag cite a render.</p></mj-text></mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-color="#ffffff" padding="16px 64px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text css-class="wysiwyg" padding="0">
          <div style="color:#F7931E;">
            <p style="margin:0;">Cascade site one — no colour of its own.</p>
            <p style="margin:0;">Cascade site two, <a href="https://tpl.org" style="color:inherit;">an inherit link</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
